// design 218 unit 1.5, spec section 1d — the instantiation DEPTH LIMIT, and
// DF-258b's refusal.
//
// `deepen<T>` instantiates ITSELF at `Wrap<T>`, so its instance set is
// infinite: `deepen<Int>`, `deepen<Wrap<Int>>`, `deepen<Wrap<Wrap<Int>>>`, on
// forever. The `n <= 0` guard is a RUNTIME condition and decides nothing about
// which instances exist.
//
// Nothing counted before this, because nothing decided the instance set as a
// whole — codegen discovered instances lazily, one at a time, so there was no
// place a chain length existed to be bounded. The compiler did not finish:
// >120 s and killed when DF-258b was filed, an `internal compiler error …
// maximum recursion depth exceeded` today, which is Python's own limit
// arriving first. A hang cannot sit in the corpus as an XFAIL, so the refusal
// ships with the fix rather than a pin preceding it.
//
// The limit is per CHAIN (64), not a global instance cap, so a program with
// thousands of shallow instantiations is untouched — and it refuses only what
// could not compile anyway.
// EXPECT: error
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: exceeds the instantiation depth limit (64)
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: deepen<Int> → deepen<Wrap<Int>>
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: has no finite instance set

struct Wrap<T> { inner: T }

func deepen<T>(x: T, n: Int) -> Int {
    if n <= 0 { return 0 }
    deepen(Wrap<T>(inner: x), n - 1)
}

func main() {
    print(deepen(1, 5))
}
